// Package loanword writes the Latin-script words of a title the way a speech recogniser for another script writes them when it hears them: katakana for Japanese, Devanagari for Hindi and Arabic letters for Arabic. Alexa's ja-JP, hi-IN and ar-SA recognisers transcribe an English or romanised title in their own script ("Superfly" as スーパーフライ, "Frozen" as फ्रोजन, "Home Alone" as هوم الون), so a library title kept in Latin letters and the request for it share no letters. Native gives the title's native-script forms, which the matcher indexes as extra aliases and the skill model carries as synonyms.
//
// A word is first read as English (english), with the spelling rules English loanwords are said by, into a sequence of phones; each language then writes the phones by its own loanword conventions. Japanese gives every consonant a vowel (epenthesis, u after most and o after t and d), lengthens the r-coloured and long vowels with ー, doubles a stop after a short vowel with ッ and writes l as the r line. Hindi writes English consonants with the Devanagari letters Hindi uses for loanwords, z as ज, and clusters with a virama; a word spelt the way Hindi film titles are romanised ("Deewaar", "Chhota") is also read letter by letter as such a romanisation. Arabic writes a consonant skeleton with the long vowels as letters, p as ب, v as ف and g as ق, as Gulf writers do. Initialisms are spelt out by letter name, and roman numerals and digits are kept as they are.
//
// The rules are Encore's own, written from descriptions of English spelling and of each language's loanword conventions; they aim at the form a recogniser most often writes, not at every spelling a speaker might choose.
package loanword
